From ea2be693e0276fdd219af55f11b18a51de2c4017 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josh Lukens <jlukens@botch.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:13:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Listeners: late copies, a narrower superseded rule, 1.5 s
 early window

From the second review of 9ed7291:

- A copy of the same bytes from another listener within 4 s of the first
  arrival is the same transmission, merged or (if already released)
  absorbed as a late copy; it was injected twice. The same listener again
  is a new transmission (listeners fold their own hop copies). A late copy
  no longer releases a pending event early.
- 'Superseded' only for a newer message for the same group from the same
  device, ordered by when the listeners heard it (the modem's copy of an
  older message can land after a listener's copy of a newer one), and
  never for replies or 0x06 cleanup reports. The cleanup of a broadcast
  (same group and command) counts as the modem having handled it.
- EARLY_S 3.0 -> 1.5 s, so a missed repeat press 2 s after a heard one is
  injected rather than taken as heard.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0157MSwwBp84p98F4Aq9dGR3
